# Line-ending policy — PHV5-002 (acceptance-matrix row A1)
#
# WHY: the reference development host runs core.autocrlf=true. Without these rules a fresh
# clone materializes executable scripts with CRLF, and a CRLF shebang line makes the interpreter
# unresolvable ("/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter", "env: 'node\r': No such file or directory").
# The hook guards and the test suite are executable scripts — `.sh`, `.js` AND `.py` — so this
# is a silent, host-dependent breakage of the plugin's safety layer.
# Markdown and JSON (`.md`, `.json`) are pinned for a DIFFERENT reason: comparators, not
# interpreters. The suite byte-compares them across locations (SPLIT-1's skill mirror, F32's
# canonical MCP block) — and on this host a selective `git checkout` re-materialized ONE copy
# CRLF while its twin stayed LF: identical blobs, clean status, divergent bytes, and a false
# positive with no symptom (2026-08-03, split step 4 boundary run). Uniform LF retires the class.
#
# That sentence named only shell scripts until CR-3 (2026-07-29). G0 selected lane N, which
# ships the guards as node scripts, and the policy silently stopped covering the files it exists
# to protect. `tests/layer1-config-wiring.sh` now asserts this rationale names both extensions,
# so it cannot go stale that way again.
#
# VERIFY (falsifying form — `git ls-files --eol` is NOT sufficient; it reports w/lf even with
# no .gitattributes present, so it passes before and after the fix):
#   git clone <repo> /tmp/eol-check && file /tmp/eol-check/scripts/*.sh
#   -> must NOT report "with CRLF line terminators"

# DEFAULT: everything git detects as text materializes LF. This policy has been burned
# twice by enumerating classes (CR-3: .js fell outside; CR-5: .py fell outside) and the
# fresh-clone check still found five stragglers (.gitattributes, .gitignore, LICENSE,
# suite.yml, a plan .txt) after .md/.json joined. A default closes the CLASS of
# "new file kind falls outside the policy" instead of adding one more entry to it.
# The repo is all-text today (verified: no tracked binaries); a future binary is
# handled by auto-detection, or freezes itself explicitly like the snapshots below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF on every platform, always.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Node scripts, same requirement (CR-3). Lane N runs the hook guards as `node scripts/tq-*.js`,
# where the shebang is decorative and CRLF is harmless — but the guards are also executable
# directly, and Wave 6's
# determinism check (PHV5-062: clean clone -> regenerate -> byte-identical) must not be
# host-dependent. Covered on the same reasoning as *.sh rather than waiting for a failure.
*.js text eol=lf

# Python, same requirement (CR-5). `tests/mutation/wave5-guards.py` is the tracked mutation
# harness — the evidence behind every "N mutations caught" claim in this plan. It landed
# OUTSIDE this policy and Layer 1 stayed green, because §14 enumerated extensions while its
# own comment claimed it derived script classes from the tree. That is the same gap CR-3 was
# ratified to close, reproduced one class later. §14 now derives by shebang as well as by
# extension, so the next interpreter cannot arrive unnoticed.
*.py text eol=lf

# Test fixtures are DATA READ BY those shell scripts — tab-separated files compared with
# `cut -f2` / `grep -qxF`. A trailing \r makes every string comparison fail silently: no
# error, just wrong answers (a fresh clone reported 5 orphan files instead of 2). Same
# core.autocrlf landmine as above, quieter failure mode. See changes/CR-2.md.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Markdown and JSON: byte-compared and jq-parsed by the suite (see the header). NOTE these
# lines must stay ABOVE the snapshots freeze — attributes are last-match-wins, and the
# snapshots' `-text` must keep overriding `*.md` or normalization would invalidate every
# recorded hash.
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# The CI failure-list contract ("CI passes IFF the failure list matches") is enforced by
# string comparison in run-all.sh; a \r on a list line would make every comparison miss.
tests/expected-failures.txt text eol=lf

# Wave 0 snapshots are an immutable historical record whose SHA-256 hashes are recorded in
# snapshots/LEDGER-HEADER.md and are the sole input to the Wave 6 reconciliation. Any line-ending
# normalization would change their bytes and invalidate every recorded hash. Freeze them: no
# conversion in either direction.
# `-eol` as well as `-text`: the `*.md` rule above sets eol=lf, and `-text` alone leaves that
# eol attribute standing (attributes resolve independently, last-match-per-attribute). Whether
# a set eol with an unset text converts is a git-version subtlety; the hash ledger's integrity
# does not get to depend on one. Unset both, explicitly.
docs/plans/2026-07-20-plugin-hardening-v5/snapshots/** -text -eol
